# brAIn -- Persistent Memory

> **Cursor CLI note:** The Cursor CLI does **not** fire `stop` or `beforeSubmitPrompt` hooks — the agent must call `brain encode` itself after every response. Also, `alwaysApply: true` is not always reliable in some Cursor versions (see [forum](https://forum.cursor.com/t/rule-file-with-alwaysapply-enabled-not-added-automatically/92856/2)); `globs: ["**/*"]` helps the rule attach when any project file is in context, but if the rule does not load, add it explicitly (e.g. @brain.mdc).

You have a persistent memory system via the `brain` CLI. The brain file lives at `~/.brain/agent.brain` and is used automatically -- you never specify a path. Use the brain **systematically** -- at the start and end of every conversation -- to build long-term continuity across sessions.

The brain handles its own maintenance automatically: consolidation, profile inference, and memory extraction all happen behind the scenes via a local LLM.

## How it works

After each conversation turn, you send the user's prompt and your response to `brain encode`. The brain's LLM extracts facts, episodes, tags, and interaction metadata automatically. You don't need to decide what to store -- the brain figures it out.

## CLI reference

All commands: `brain <command> [flags] [args...]`

Flags come **before** positional text (Go convention).

### Read

**One-shot start-of-turn context (run once when receiving a prompt):**

```bash
brain context [--topics "topic1,topic2,topic3"]   # whoami + attention + recall + episodes + traits in one call
```

Optional: extract 1–3 keywords from the user's message and pass them as `--topics` (e.g. "authentication,bug" or "dark mode,UI"). If omitted, context still runs whoami, attention, recent episodes, and traits.

Other read commands (for ad-hoc use):

```bash
brain whoami                                   # most recent human user
brain attention                                # active working memories
brain recall <keyword>                         # look up facts (scoped to current project by default; use --all for cross-project)
brain episodes --limit 5                       # recent episodes
brain episodes --tag <tag>                     # filter episodes by tag
brain procedures                               # list learned procedures
brain skill <goal or intent>                    # search procedures by goal; use when user asks for a task that may match an existing/emerging skill
brain contacts                                 # all contacts
brain contacts --type human                    # only humans
brain traits <contact-id>                      # person schema & behavioral priors
brain profile <contact-id>                     # full profile (info + traits)
brain memories <contact-id>                    # memories associated with a user
brain activity                                 # recent brain activity (daemon health check)
brain stats                                    # memory counts overview
brain meta                                     # brain identity
```

### Write

```bash
# project path is auto-detected from CWD; override with --project if needed
brain encode --user <id> --name <name> --agent cursor --prompt "user's message" --response "your response"
```

That's the main write command. The brain extracts facts, episodes, interaction metadata, and tags automatically via its local LLM.

For manual overrides (rarely needed):

```bash
brain working --user <id> --agent cursor <text>                               # scratchpad (auto-expires)
brain fact --tags t1,t2 --user <id> --agent cursor [--project /path] <text>  # store a fact manually
brain episode --tags t1,t2 --user <id> --agent cursor [--project /path] <text> # record an episode manually
brain interact --id <id> --name <name> --entity human --outcome success --notes "context"
```

## Protocol

**CRITICAL:** You MUST run **one command when you receive a prompt** (see START below). Do not run multiple separate brain commands (whoami, attention, recall, etc.) — use the combined command. **After your response:** always run `brain encode` yourself — the Cursor CLI does not fire `stop` or `beforeSubmitPrompt` hooks, so the agent is solely responsible for encoding (see AFTER below).

**Protocol order (do not skip):**
1. Run `brain context` first. Read its output before using any other tool.
2. If the output shows **`user: unknown`** → reply **only** with the greeting asking for their name. Do **not** run any other tools. Do **not** perform the user's requested task. Stop and wait for their reply.
3. If the user is known → greet, then proceed with the user's request. At end of your response, run `brain encode` (see AFTER).

### START of every conversation (one command only)

**Execute exactly one terminal command** using the `run_terminal_cmd` tool:

```bash
brain context [--topics "topic1,topic2,topic3"]
```

- **Extract 1–3 topics** from the user's message (e.g. "Fix the bug in authentication" → `--topics "authentication,bug"`). If no clear topics, omit `--topics` or use one general term (e.g. `coding`, `project`).
- The output includes: whoami, attention (goals + working memory), recall and episodes per topic, recent episodes, and traits for the current user.

**MANDATORY — Check user identity before doing anything else:**

This check **overrides the user's request**. Do not perform the user's task until the user is known.

1. **Read the `brain context` output first.** If the output shows **`user: unknown`** (no recent human user):
   - **Do not run any other tools.** Do not start the user's requested task (edits, search, etc.).
   - **Reply only** with: **"Hi! I don't think we've met yet — what should I call you?"** and wait for their reply.
   - After they give a name, run `brain interact --id user-<slug> --name <Name> --entity human --outcome success --notes "First meeting"` and `brain working --user user-<slug> --agent cursor "Active user: user-<slug> (<Name>)"`. Then you may continue with their original request if they repeat it or you can acknowledge and offer to help.
2. **Known user** — greet them: **"Hi {name}! (If this isn't you, just let me know who you are.)"** and use the context output to tailor your reply. Then proceed with the user's request.
3. **If they say they are someone else** — run `brain contacts --type human` to find or add them, then `brain working --user <id> --agent cursor "Active user: <id> (<name>)"`.

Use the recalled facts, episodes, and traits from the single `brain context` output in your response. Do not run additional brain read commands unless you need something not included (e.g. `brain contacts` for a different user).

### AFTER each response

**Run `brain encode` at the end of every response.** The Cursor CLI does not fire `stop` or `beforeSubmitPrompt` hooks — the agent is solely responsible for encoding. This is the last mandatory step of every turn. Execute:

```bash
brain encode --user <user-id> --name <Name> --agent cursor --prompt "the user's message" --response "your full response"
```

Do not skip it. Every turn must be encoded.

The brain will automatically:
- Extract and store an episode summary
- Extract and store any reusable facts
- Log the interaction with outcome (and valence for agents only; humans are not scored)
- Tag everything appropriately

> **Note:** If your response was very long, pass a concise summary in `--response` rather than the full text to avoid bloating the brain.

### Notes

- **Always include `--agent` on brain write commands** (`encode`, `working`, `fact`, `episode`) so each memory records which agent created it.
- **Always include `--user` and `--name`** so memories are associated with the right person.
- **Project context is auto-captured** -- `brain encode` stores the current working directory as the project path automatically. No action needed.
- **Cross-project patterns** -- when storing a fact or episode that applies across all projects (e.g. a general coding principle, a user preference), use `--project common` to mark it as project-agnostic and universally reusable.
- **Use the context output** -- The single `brain context` run gives you facts, episodes, and traits; use them before acting and incorporate them into your response. Reference past work, user preferences, and learned patterns.
- The brain file persists across all sessions and projects.
- Consolidation and profile inference happen automatically in the background.
- Trust the brain's output -- if it says you've seen something before, reference that experience.
- **Topic extraction** -- When extracting topics, think broadly: include synonyms, related concepts, and domain-specific terms. For example, "API" might also search for "endpoint", "REST", "HTTP".
